# sh(1)

## NAME

sh - the command shell

## SYNOPSIS

    sh [-eux] [-c command [name [arg...]]]
    sh [-eux] script [arg...]
    sh [-eux] [-s] [arg...]

## DESCRIPTION

A POSIX-style shell: pipelines, lists, `if`, `while`, `for`, `case`, functions, redirections, here-documents, `$(...)`, parameter expansion and globbing. On a terminal it reads commands interactively with line editing, completion (Tab) and history (`~/.sh_history`).

Built-in commands: `:` `.` `eval` `exec` `exit` `export` `readonly` `return` `set` `shift` `unset` `break` `continue` `local` `history` `true` `false` `echo` `printf` `cd` `pwd` `read` `test` `[` `wait` `jobs` `command` `type` `source`.

## OPTIONS

- `-c`: run the command string
- `-e`: exit when a command fails
- `-u`: treat unset variables as errors
- `-x`: print commands before running them
- `-s`: read commands from standard input

## ENVIRONMENT

- `PATH`: where commands are looked up (default `/bin:/sbin`)
- `HOME`: the directory for `cd` without an argument
- `PWD`: the working directory

